---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "How to Split a Bill Fairly, Including the Tip - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "How to work out the tip, the total and each person's share of a restaurant or group bill, and how to handle uneven orders.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-to-split-a-bill-fairly">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Everyday maths</p>
    <h1>How to Split a Bill Fairly, Including the Tip</h1>
    <p class="lead">A shared bill is easy to split evenly, but the tip, sales tax and uneven orders make it messy. A simple sequence keeps everyone paying a fair share.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>Step 1: add the tip to the bill</h2>
<p>Work out the tip on the bill before tax, if the menu prices include tax you should use the total. For a bill of 60 with an 18% tip, the tip is 10.80 and the total is 70.80.</p>
<h2>Step 2: divide by the number of people</h2>
<p>Divide the total by the number of people sharing it. Three people paying 70.80 each pay 23.60.</p>
<h2>When the orders are uneven</h2>
<p>If one person had a much more expensive meal, split the items each person ordered, then share the tip and any tax in proportion. Calculating each share from its own items avoids arguments about who had the wine.</p>
<h2>Rounding</h2>
<p>Amounts rarely divide exactly. Round up for one person, or agree in advance who covers the cent difference, so no one ends up short.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/tip-calculator">Tip Calculator</a> works out the tip, the total and each person's share as you change the bill, the tip percentage and the number of people.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>A bill of 60 with an 18% tip: the tip is 60 × 0.18 = 10.80, so the total is 70.80. Split three ways, each person pays 70.80 ÷ 3 = 23.60.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Should the tip be on the bill before or after tax?</summary><p>Many people tip on the amount before tax. Choose one method and use it consistently for your group.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What if people ordered different amounts?</summary><p>Work out each person's share from their own items, then add their portion of the tip and tax.</p></details>
        <details><summary>How do I handle rounding?</summary><p>Decide in advance who covers the cent difference, so no one is short.</p></details>
      </section>
